 "payload": {
  "question": "CIP-007-6 R2 Part 2.3 gives a choice of three actions within 35 calendar days. Which of them do our procedures allow, and does anything we have written narrow that choice?",
  "ref": "CIP-007-6 R2 Part 2.3",
  "answer": "## What the standard grants\n\nCIP-007-6 R2 Part 2.3 (csection-c36c38b8842c8bc217ac) gives the Responsible Entity a **choice of three actions**, to be taken \"within 35 calendar days of the evaluation completion\":\n\n1. Apply the applicable patches; or\n2. Create a dated mitigation plan; or\n3. Revise an existing mitigation plan.\n\nThe Guidelines and Technical Basis (csection-7d6ba196ae0e7c219624) confirms the intent: \"the entity either installs the patch or documents (either through the creation of a new or update of an existing mitigation plan) what they are going to do to mitigate the vulnerability and when they are going to do so.\" It also notes that timeframes in mitigation plans \"do not have to be designated as a particular calendar day but can have event designations such as 'at next scheduled outage of at least two days duration.'\"\n\n## What the operator's procedures allow\n\nThe operator's **LSPG Security Patch Management Procedure V11.0** (effective 2026-07-31) addresses Part 2.3 in two operative sections:\n\n**Section 3.5 \u2013 Patch Implementation Process** (isection-c75f69c881d6a214ff52):\n\n> \"For all identified applicable security or potential security patches, within thirty-five (35) calendar days of the evaluation, one of the following actions shall be taken:\n> 3.5.1.1 RFC approval and installation of applicable patches\n> 3.5.1.2 Creation of a dated mitigation plan\n> 3.5.1.3 Revision of an existing mitigation plan\"\n\n**Section 3.6 \u2013 Patch Mitigation Plan Management** (isection-12bd999ecfa8bf61d440):\n\n> \"Applicable security patches determined not able to be installed within 35 calendar days of evaluation are added to a mitigation plan by creating a dated mitigation plan or revising an existing mitigation plan within the Change Management Solution.\"\n\nThe **CIP Cyber Security Policy V13.0** (isection-87d00aedd4cf1f41f0cd, \u00a73.4.2.1) restates the same three actions at the policy level.\n\n## Does anything narrow the choice?\n\n**No.** The operator's procedure preserves all three permitted actions as a genuine either/or/or choice. Section 3.5.1 lists the same three options the standard grants, in the same order, with the same 35-calendar-day clock. The operator has not hardcoded a single path, has not eliminated any of the three, and has not imposed a shorter internal deadline than the standard's 35 days.\n\nThe one procedural addition is that the \"apply the patch\" path is routed through an RFC (Request for Change) per the OT Change Management procedure (\u00a73.5.2). This is an internal governance step for how the patch is installed; it does not remove the option or condition it in a way that would make it unavailable within the 35-day window.\n\nThe traceability appendix (isection-e450e773b7d8578062c6, \u00a75.1) maps Part 2.3 to \"Section 3.5 & 3.6\" and reproduces the regulatory text. As a cross-reference table it confirms the operator's own mapping but is not itself an operative procedure.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe operator's written procedures allow all three actions the standard grants \u2014 apply, create a mitigation plan, or revise an existing mitigation plan \u2014 within the same 35-calendar-day window. Nothing in the operator's documents narrows the choice to fewer than three options or shortens the deadline. The operator is using the full latitude NERC provides.\n\n**Sections I did not read and why:** The five `control-*` references returned by `compliance_links` (control-fc5bc3b52d308010a770, control-b665d2babd3f620f42b0, control-807b29da377cdd61aef7, control-e4d5b8fc83c96c642da4, control-d19ed07cdcf810e1db80, control-d040d7b23c8e10948efc, control-73927b287e7bce4ee51b, control-b1e3bf00f72d4055aa6b, control-d088d27219e9cd57d5b0, control-cea10da722f207009f31, control-c8879fbee275, control-138bba8cb005) are folder-cartesian or semantic-reading candidates with zero confidence and no recorded text; they are not operator sections I can read as verbatim text, and the five `isection-*` references I did read are the substantive operator documents. No operator notes exist for this subject.",
